      "component_id": "concern_001",
      "text": "If an online store delivers a rice cooker that is defective, the consumer may demand repair, replacement, refund or any other remedy that the Consumer Act (RA 7394) provides. Under RA 11967 Sec. 20 (and the same rule in JAO 24‑03 Sec. 37), when the consumer opts for a replacement or a refund, the merchant can ask for the original unit to be sent back at no cost to the consumer, and the return must be made within a reasonable period unless the parties have agreed on another timeline. If the defect is not fixed within 30 days, RA 7394 Art. 100 lets the consumer choose one of three alternatives: a replacement of the same kind, a full refund with monetary updating, or a proportional price reduction. The law’s reach stops at these statutory remedies; it does not create any additional duties for the merchant beyond returning the goods or paying the chosen remedy.",
