      "text": "In a regular retail sale, a seller commits a deceptive act when he makes a false representation about the product—such as claiming it is new, of a certain quality, or has a benefit it does not actually have. Art. 50 of the Consumer Act (RA 7394) expressly lists these misrepresentations as prohibited, whether they occur before, during, or after the transaction. The rule only defines what counts as a deceptive practice; it does not itself grant a specific remedy, which must be pursued under other consumer‑protection provisions. Thus the scope of this provision ends at identifying the deceptive act; it does not prescribe how the consumer must be compensated.",
      "evidence": [
