      "text": "If an online store delivers a rice cooker that is defective, the consumer may invoke the remedies set out in Republic Act No. 11967 Sec. 20 and the Joint Administrative Order No. 24‑03. The law gives the consumer the right to ask for repair, replacement, or refund. When the consumer chooses replacement or refund, the merchant may require the return of the original unit at no cost to the consumer, unless the parties have agreed otherwise. If a refund has already been paid and the consumer cannot return the unit because of the consumer’s fault, the consumer must reimburse the merchant proportionally. Under Republic Act No. 7394 Art. 100, if the defect is not corrected within a reasonable period, the consumer may also elect a replacement, a refund with monetary updating, or a proportional price reduction. The legal scope ends once the consumer has selected a remedy and, if required, returned the original product.",
